      "compare": "exact",
      "hints": [
        "Brute force over every start and end is O(n^2). Instead, ask: what is the best run that ends exactly at index i?",
        "That best run either extends the best run ending at i - 1 or starts fresh at nums[i]: cur = max(x, cur + x).",
        "Track the best cur seen so far, and start both values at nums[0], not 0, so an all-negative list works."
      ],
      "solution": {
        "javascript": "function maxSubArray(nums) {\n  let cur = nums[0]\n  let best = nums[0]\n  for (let i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    cur = Math.max(nums[i], cur + nums[i])\n    best = Math.max(best, cur)\n  }\n  return best\n}\n",
        "python": "def max_sub_array(nums):\n    cur = best = nums[0]\n    for x in nums[1:]:\n        cur = max(x, cur + x)      # extend the run, or start fresh at x\n        best = max(best, cur)\n    return best\n"
      },
      "explanation": "Kadane's algorithm keeps cur, the best sum of a run ending at the current index. A run with a negative total can only lower what follows, so cur restarts at x whenever cur + x < x. The answer is the best cur seen anywhere. Starting from nums[0] keeps the run non-empty.",
      "complexity": {
        "time": "O(n)",
        "space": "O(1)"
      }
