      "hints": [
        "If you can reach index j, you can also reach every index before it. So what single number describes everything reachable?",
        "Scan left to right, keeping farthest = the largest index reachable so far, updated with i + nums[i].",
        "If you ever reach an index i with i > farthest, it is unreachable and so is the end."
      ],
      "solution": {
        "javascript": "function canJump(nums) {\n  let farthest = 0\n  for (let i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {\n    if (i > farthest) return false\n    farthest = Math.max(farthest, i + nums[i])\n    if (farthest >= nums.length - 1) return true\n  }\n  return true\n}\n",
        "python": "def can_jump(nums):\n    farthest = 0\n    for i, step in enumerate(nums):\n        if i > farthest:          # a gap: i is unreachable\n            return False\n        farthest = max(farthest, i + step)\n        if farthest >= len(nums) - 1:\n            return True\n    return True\n"
      },
      "explanation": "The reachable indexes always form one unbroken stretch from 0 to farthest, because any jump may be shorter than its maximum. A single scan extends that stretch with i + nums[i] and fails as soon as it meets an index beyond it.",
