      "hints": [
        "Sort the intervals by start. Then an interval can only overlap the block you are currently building.",
        "Keep a result list. If the next interval starts at or before the last block's end, extend that block; otherwise append a new block.",
        "When extending, use the larger of the two ends: an interval can sit entirely inside the current block. In JavaScript, sort with (a, b) => a[0] - b[0]."
      ],
      "solution": {
        "javascript": "function merge(intervals) {\n  const sorted = [...intervals].sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0])\n  const merged = [[...sorted[0]]]\n  for (let i = 1; i < sorted.length; i++) {\n    const [start, end] = sorted[i]\n    const last = merged[merged.length - 1]\n    if (start <= last[1]) last[1] = Math.max(last[1], end)\n    else merged.push([start, end])\n  }\n  return merged\n}\n",
        "python": "def merge(intervals):\n    intervals = sorted(intervals, key=lambda iv: iv[0])\n    merged = [intervals[0][:]]\n    for start, end in intervals[1:]:\n        last = merged[-1]\n        if start <= last[1]:\n            last[1] = max(last[1], end)\n        else:\n            merged.append([start, end])\n    return merged\n"
      },
